4. Evidence Assessment

[V] Observed

The local simulation successfully used Ed25519 asymmetric signing and verification.

The application validated responses using the public verification key. Tests demonstrated rejection of altered payloads, unrelated signatures, incorrect request bindings and unavailable-authority conditions.

[I] Interpretation

Asymmetric signing provides a useful architectural separation between the authority that creates approval signatures and the application that verifies them.

Unlike a shared HMAC secret, the application does not need the private signing key to verify an authority response.

This can reduce the impact of compromising an application component, provided private-key custody and the authority boundary are genuinely protected.

5. Limitations

- The test ran in one local Python environment.
- Authority and application were separate objects, not independently secured processes or services.
- Private and public keys were generated in memory.
- No hardware security module, secure enclave, production key vault or external key-management service was tested.
- No key rotation, revocation or durable key lifecycle was tested in this milestone.
- Replay protection uses an in-memory set and is not durable across restarts.
- No remote API, TLS transport or production payment system was tested.
- No independent penetration test or third-party security audit was performed.

6. Conclusion

AG-C135: PASS — All 20 asymmetric verification checks passed.

The simulation demonstrates that an application can verify authority responses using a public key without holding the authority's private signing key.

The results support further research into independently secured recovery authorities, protected signing-key custody and durable replay prevention.

These findings apply only to the tested local simulation and do not establish production security.

